.TH XSIXEL 1 "November 7, 2026" "XLibre @version@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
Xsixel \- X server displaying a desktop in a SIXEL terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B Xsixel
.RI [ :display ]
.RI [ option " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Xsixel
is an XLibre KDrive X server that renders an X desktop as SIXEL images
in a terminal and receives keyboard and mouse input from that terminal.
Run it in the foreground with standard input and output attached to a
SIXEL-capable terminal, locally or over SSH.
X applications connect to its display in the usual way.
For example,
.B Xsixel :9 \-exec ratpoison
starts a desktop on display
.BR :9 .
No host X server is required for SIXEL output.
.PP
The default renderer uses libsixel and updates damaged areas of the desktop.
An optional Chafa renderer is available in builds configured with
.BR \-Dchafa=true .
Normal shutdown erases the displayed image, moves the text cursor to the
top-left corner, and restores the terminal display and input modes.
This also applies when the
.B \-exec
command finishes; with
.BR \-keep ,
command completion alone does not erase the image.
.SH OPTIONS
See
.BR Xserver (1)
and
.BR Xkdrive (1)
for common server options.
The following options control the terminal backend:
.TP
.BI \-screen " width" x height
Set a fixed logical X desktop size in pixels.
Without this option, Xsixel determines the size from terminal pixel and
cell dimensions and follows terminal resizes through RandR.
It initially uses the terminal's reported pixel size, or 640x480 if
unavailable, and corrects it as terminal query replies arrive.
Explicit dimensions remain fixed when the terminal changes size.
.TP
.BI \-exec " command"
After server startup, run
.I command
using
.BR /bin/sh\ \-c ,
with
.B DISPLAY
set to the new server's display.
Quote commands containing spaces so they form one argument.
Command output and exit diagnostics go to a private log; see
.BR FILES .
.IP
By default, Xsixel exits when this shell command finishes, including
nonzero exit or termination by a signal, even if other X connections remain.
Keep the session application in the foreground:
backgrounding it allows the shell to finish early.
The command's exit status is recorded in the log rather than used as
Xsixel's exit status.
.TP
.B \-keep
.TQ
.B \-keep-server
Keep Xsixel running after the
.B \-exec
command finishes.
The two names are equivalent and have no effect without
.BR \-exec .
An explicit
.B \-terminate
still terminates the server when the last X client disconnects.
.TP
.BI \-mouse-protocol " sgr|dec"
Select terminal mouse reporting.
The default,
.BR sgr ,
requests pixel coordinates when supported and falls back to character-cell
coordinates when the terminal reports pixel mode as unavailable.
tmux and GNU screen use cell coordinates.
Cell reporting limits pointer precision to character cells.
The
.B dec
alternative requires DEC locator support in the terminal;
use the default SGR protocol in multiplexers.
.TP
.B \-iterm2
Force iTerm2 compatibility rendering when automatic identification is
unavailable.
Normally iTerm2 is detected from the startup environment or a terminal
identity query, including over SSH and through GNU screen.
.TP
.B \-konsole
Force Konsole rendering when automatic identification is unavailable.
Konsole is detected from
.B KONSOLE_VERSION
or its startup identity reply, including through tmux and GNU screen.
Opaque images include the framebuffer from its origin through the damage,
rounded up to whole cells and complete six-pixel bands in height.
Unknown geometry uses full images.
This option does not enable iTerm2 geometry or Retina scaling.
.TP
.BI \-iterm2-scale " auto|1|2"
Enable iTerm2 compatibility and select image enlargement.
The default,
.BR auto ,
selects scale 1 or 2 from iTerm2's cell-size and Retina-scale reply;
without a usable reply it keeps scale 1.
An explicit 1 or 2 overrides the detected scale.
At scale 2, each logical X pixel becomes a 2x2 block of image pixels.
For example, a 1120x850 image area displays a 560x425 logical desktop.
.B \-screen
always specifies logical dimensions.
Scale 2 requires the libsixel backend.
tmux always uses scale 1.
.TP
.B \-iterm2-full-redraw
Enable iTerm2 compatibility and send full images instead of partial row
updates.
Use this when sharing a GNU screen session between viewers with different
character-cell dimensions.
The rendering mode and scale apply to all viewers of the same Xsixel server.
A viewer attaching after startup is not identified automatically;
specify a scale explicitly if necessary.
.TP
.BI \-backend " libsixel|chafa"
Select the renderer.
The default is
.BR libsixel .
.B chafa
is accepted only when Chafa support was enabled at build time.
Chafa uses full redraws and does not support scale 2.
.TP
.B \-help
Display server command-line help, including common KDrive options.
.SH TERMINAL SIZE AND INPUT
Automatic sizing uses terminal queries at startup and after resize
notifications, without waiting for replies before starting the server.
Resize notifications are coalesced for 100 milliseconds.
Inside tmux, the outer terminal's cell dimensions are combined with the
pane's own rows and columns.
If size reports are missing or invalid, the current size is retained.
.PP
iTerm2 Retina image dimensions and mouse coordinates can use different
units; automatic scale detection accounts for this.
Windows Terminal's reported SIXEL pixels are virtual pixels and need not
equal physical display pixels.
.PP
Mouse motion, dragging, the first three buttons, and vertical and horizontal
wheel events are supported by SGR reporting.
If an outer xterm suppresses mouse events, start it with an empty
.B disallowedMouseOps
resource, as shown in
.BR EXAMPLES .
.PP
When built with libX11 and libXfixes, Xsixel can hide the outer X terminal's
pointer using its original
.B DISPLAY
and
.BR WINDOWID .
The pointer is restored on leaving that window and on server exit.
Otherwise it requests terminal pointer hiding, which some terminals ignore.
.SH MULTIPLEXERS
.SS tmux
When
.B TMUX
is set, Xsixel automatically uses passthrough to the outer terminal.
tmux does not need to be built with SIXEL support.
Before starting Xsixel, enable passthrough for the pane and focus events:
.PP
.nf
tmux set-option \-p allow-passthrough on
tmux set-option \-g focus-events on
.fi
.PP
Xsixel checks both settings and exits with setup instructions if either
is disabled; it does not change the configuration itself.
Passthrough values
.B on
and
.B all
are accepted.
Use a single pane or zoom it with
.BR "Ctrl-b z" ,
preferably with the status line disabled:
.PP
.nf
tmux set-option status off
.fi
.PP
Returning to the Xsixel window triggers a full redraw through focus events.
Automatic terminal identification does not switch tmux to iTerm2 rendering;
its iTerm2 cell-size query is used only for geometry.
.PP
Konsole images must fit in one passthrough packet.  In Konsole mode,
Xsixel checks the server's
.B input-buffer-size
once, falling back to the fixed 1 MiB capacity of older tmux versions.
If an encoded image exceeds the capacity, Xsixel restores the terminal and
exits with instructions to increase the limit and restart, for example:
.PP
.nf
tmux set-option \-s input-buffer-size 8388608
.fi
.PP
Use the value requested by the diagnostic.  To persist it, add
.B set \-s input-buffer-size 8388608
to the tmux configuration.  Older tmux versions without this option need
an upgrade, or a direct/GNU screen session for large Konsole images.
No tmux settings are changed automatically.
.SS GNU screen
When
.B STY
is set, Xsixel automatically uses GNU screen's DCS passthrough.
No SIXEL build option or passthrough setting is required in screen.
Use one full-screen region without hardstatus or caption lines.
Xsixel resends a full image periodically, restoring the display within
about two seconds after window switching or reattachment.
For example:
.PP
.nf
screen Xsixel :9 \-exec ratpoison
.fi
.PP
GNU screen 5.0.1 does not forward pixel-only size changes when rows and
columns remain unchanged, such as some font-size changes.
It requires a screen-side pixel-resize fix for Xsixel to follow those changes.
.SS Limitations
The outer terminal must support SIXEL and mouse reporting.
Split-pane or split-region clipping and nested multiplexers are unsupported.
If both
.B TMUX
and
.B STY
are set, tmux takes precedence.
.SH RUNTIME RENDERING MODE
With the libsixel backend, the root-window property
.B _XSIXEL_RENDER_MODE
selects the drawing policy.
The initial value reflects startup options and terminal identification.
Its type must be
.BR STRING ,
format 8, with the exact value
.BR fast ,
.BR iterm2 ,
or
.BR konsole .
.B fast
uses cropped damage updates.
.B iterm2
uses opaque, full-width character-row updates when cell geometry permits,
otherwise full images, to avoid partial-image artifacts in iTerm2.
.B konsole
uses opaque prefixes from the origin, aligned to whole cells, and disables
error diffusion to preserve background colors between updates.
Explicit rendering options and property writes override automatic detection.
.PP
Read the current mode or change it using
.BR xprop (1):
.PP
.nf
xprop \-display :9 \-root _XSIXEL_RENDER_MODE
xprop \-display :9 \-root \-f _XSIXEL_RENDER_MODE 8s \e
    \-set _XSIXEL_RENDER_MODE iterm2
xprop \-display :9 \-root \-f _XSIXEL_RENDER_MODE 8s \e
    \-set _XSIXEL_RENDER_MODE fast
.fi
.PP
A mode change schedules a full redraw; writing the same value does not.
Invalid values, deletion and property rotation are rejected.
Properties on ordinary client windows have no effect.
This control changes rendering policy, not terminal identity or scale,
and applies to every viewer until server restart.
Use
.B xrefresh \-display :9
for an explicit full redraw without changing modes.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B KONSOLE_VERSION
Identify Konsole when nonempty.  A startup terminal identity query can also
select this compatibility mode when SSH omits the environment hint.
.TP
.B TMUX
Select tmux passthrough when nonempty.
.TP
.B TMUX_PANE
Identify the current tmux pane when checking its passthrough setting.
This variable is normally supplied by tmux together with
.BR TMUX .
.TP
.B STY
Select GNU screen passthrough when nonempty and tmux is not selected.
.TP
.B TERM_PROGRAM
The value
.B iTerm.app
identifies iTerm2 at startup.
.TP
.B LC_TERMINAL
The value
.B iTerm2
also identifies iTerm2 at startup.
These variables are optional when terminal identity queries work.
.TP
.B DISPLAY, WINDOWID
Identify the outer X terminal for host-pointer hiding when that support
is available.
The
.B \-exec
command receives the new Xsixel display instead of the original
.BR DISPLAY .
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Directory for private command logs.
If unset or empty,
.B /tmp
is used.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $TMPDIR/Xsixel-N-exec-XXXXXX
Private log for each
.B \-exec
launch, where
.I N
is the display number and the final characters are unique.
It contains command stdout, stderr and exit diagnostics, is readable and
writable only by its owner, and remains available after command completion.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start a window manager, with automatic desktop size and iTerm2 detection:
.PP
.nf
Xsixel :9 \-exec ratpoison
.fi
.PP
Connect an application from another shell:
.PP
.nf
xterm \-display :9
.fi
.PP
Keep the server after its initial application exits:
.PP
.nf
Xsixel :9 \-keep \-exec xterm
.fi
.PP
Use a fixed logical desktop with explicit Retina enlargement:
.PP
.nf
Xsixel :9 \-screen 560x425 \-iterm2-scale 2 \-exec ratpoison
.fi
.PP
Start an outer SIXEL-capable xterm with mouse reporting enabled and a
larger graphics limit, then run Xsixel inside it:
.PP
.nf
xterm \-xrm 'XTerm*decTerminalID: vt340' \e
    \-xrm 'XTerm*numColorRegisters: 256' \e
    \-xrm 'XTerm*maxGraphicSize: 4096x4096' \e
    \-xrm 'XTerm*disallowedMouseOps:'
.fi
.SH CAVEATS
xterm's graphics-size limit is independent of its window size and defaults
to 1000x1000.
If the X desktop has the correct dimensions but its right or bottom portion
is clipped, increase
.B maxGraphicSize
when starting the outer xterm.
.PP
tmux can omit resize notifications when font pixels change without a
change in pane rows or columns.
An iTerm2 with tmux redraw defect after terminal resizing is also known;
correct geometry detection alone does not ensure an intact image in that case.
